the konevets icon of the mother of god konevits konevskaya golubitskaya theotokos the oca associates the konevets icon with arsenios’s return from mount athos and the foundation on lake ladoga. the selected record identifies a late-fifteenth- or sixteenth-century icon in finland. mary holds the child, who has a small bird. its material is recorded as tempera on canvas and wood; the whole panel remains visible. the published tradition gives 1393 for arsenios’s return with an athonite icon. it describes the island monastery, dangers during later conflicts, and transfers to novgorod before the icon’s return in 1799. the selected artwork record supplies a different, object-specific date; an origin tradition is not used to redate this historic panel. the oca describes the image’s continuing place in the monastery’s life and reports july 10 as its commemoration. it admits that precise information about the event behind that date is lacking. the profile preserves that limit rather than inventing a detailed military miracle. and when the days of her purification according to the law of moses were accomplished, they brought him to jerusalem, to present him to the lord;

(as it is written in the law of the lord, every male that openeth the womb shall be called holy to the lord;)

and to offer a sacrifice according to that which is said in the law of the lord, a pair of turtledoves, or two young pigeons.

catherine of alexandria katherine aikaterina saints saint catherine’s parish explains the saint’s imperial garments, martyr’s cross, books, celestial sphere, and wheel as identifying attributes in her iconography. the selected historic sinai icon shows catherine enthroned with books, instruments, and narrative scenes. its whole composition is retained, including the surrounding episodes; it is not cropped to conceal contextual details. the parish’s published life recounts catherine’s learned defense of christianity and her martyrdom, then connects her relics with the sinai monastery. these are its hagiographic traditions. the selected icon is an illustration of her later veneration, not a contemporary record of the trial. the parish explicitly associates the books and celestial sphere with knowledge and wisdom, and the wheel with martyrdom. its explanation of her courtly clothing belongs to that published iconographic reading. the symbols are attributed to the parish rather than invented for the app. but god hath chosen the foolish things of the world to confound the wise; and god hath chosen the weak things of the world to confound the things which are mighty;

and base things of the world, and things which are despised, hath god chosen, yea, and things which are not, to bring to nought things that are:

that no flesh should glory in his presence.
